Subject: [PATCH 01/12] R80b, part 2, patch 1 (cld-advisor, at main's word in
 M14; Review 80, a reader's findings F1, F3 and F4, each checked by main with
 a plant of its own that passes): THE LOCK OF B1881 HOLDS THE SCRIPT WHOLE.

WHAT STOOD. The lock read the script's text in places: two conditions word for word, the beginning of two expressions, the shape of one kind of `or`. Main's plants weaken the script elsewhere and leave what it prints as it was: the two mended conditions taken over empty lists; a check made unable to fail by an `or` on a length that is never 0; no sequence read, the least typed and the two counts typed behind the beginning the lock pins. The run compared with the committed files is then the same, and the second methods agree with it. All 15 tests passed on each.

WHAT IS CHANGED. One test holds the script by the digest of its bytes, so that any change to it is a change in two places. The three readings of the script's text that the lock made before are functions of a text now, word for word, each with a dated note; the two tests whose bodies they were assert what the functions return.

THE PLANTED CASES, in the patch's own test: main's five plants as main wrote them, the two that are answered by the next two patches among them. Each is put to the readings of before and passes them, which is the finding; each changes the digest. A plant that B1895's lock holds is refused by the readings of before, so they are not blind to everything.

WHAT IT CANNOT HOLD, said in the test: a change made to the script and to the digest together.

The lock: 16 tests pass. Main's own script of plants, run on the series' last patch: none of the five passes. 0 of 19.
